            <ArticleTitle>Allergic diseases, gene-environment interactions.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>10-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1398-9995.2011.02622.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Allergic asthma develops in part from dysregulation of the innate and adaptive immune functions, particularly an imbalance in the Th2-driven adaptive immune response. This dysregulation is the result of complex interactions between genes and environment. These interactions occur both pre- and postnatally, providing opportunities for early interventions in immunological programming.</AbstractText>
